# web/.dockerignore
#
# R199'S ARGUMENT, APPLIED TO A BUILD CONTEXT. gate:clone proves the tree works
# on a machine that is not mine by CLONING it, so .env and node_modules are
# absent BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by a scrub list somebody maintains. A
# docker build cannot clone, so this file is that scrub list - and it is
# written to fail in the safe direction: everything generated is excluded and
# regenerated inside the image, so a stale artefact on this machine can never
# become the one that ships.
#
# THE THREE THAT MATTER MOST, and why each is here rather than merely tidy:
#
#   .env*          a secret in a build context is a secret in the image, in a
#                  layer, readable by anyone who pulls it. It is never needed:
#                  every value this product takes at runtime is read at
#                  runtime, and the entrypoint refuses to start without them.
#   node_modules   the host's tree is Windows-resolved and may carry optional
#                  binaries for the wrong platform. The image installs from
#                  the lockfile, which is the same claim gate:clone makes.
#   public/fonts   GENERATED by `pnpm build` (scripts/copy-fonts.mjs). Copying
#                  the host's would let the image ship fonts that the build no
#                  longer produces - the exact drift R181 names, in a binary.

node_modules
.next
.next-visual-*
.turbo
out

# Generated by the build. Never copied in.
public/fonts
public/opengraph-image.png.tmp

# Secrets and local state.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Test output and baselines: the image serves the product, it does not test it.
test-results
playwright-report
coverage
tests/visual/baselines
tests/visual/output
*.tsbuildinfo

# Editor and OS noise.
.vscode
.idea
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.log
